## Why This Is Asked

Understanding data leakage and proper evaluation on small datasets is critical for reliable models. This question tests practical thinking, not theory alone, and guards against tempting but flawed splits that could invalidate results.

## Key Concepts

- Data leakage and temporal splits
- Baseline models and feature encoding
- Evaluation metrics and calibration
- Time-aware validation strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple logistic regression gradient step (conceptual)
function step(w, b, x, y, lr) {
  const z = 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-(dot(w, x) + b)));
  const error = y - z;
  // Update weights and bias
  for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) {
    w[i] += lr * error * x[i];
  }
  b += lr * error;
  return { w, b };
}
```

This example demonstrates the core gradient descent logic while keeping the implementation minimal and focused on the mathematical foundation.

## Why This Question Is Asked
Evaluates the ability to design real-world, latency-constrained explainability systems that balance privacy and governance requirements in high-stakes ML environments.

## Key Concepts
- Per-instance explanations under strict latency constraints
- Privacy-preserving attribution without raw feature exposure
- Caching, bucketing, and surrogate models for performance optimization
- Drift monitoring and governance compliance

## Code Example
```javascript
function attribution(event) {
  const bucket = features.map(f => bucketize(f.value, f.schema));
  const fingerprint = hash(bucket);
  const cached = cache.get(fingerprint);
  if (cached) return cached;
  const attribution = surrogate.predict(bucket);
  cache.set(fingerprint, attribution);
  return attribution;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Production ML systems must adapt to concept drift and data skew without sacrificing stability. This question probes concrete, actionable strategies beyond theory.

## Key Concepts

- Concept drift detection
- Sliding window retraining
- Score calibration and monitoring
- Safe deployment and rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal drift check sketch (conceptual)
function detectDrift(scores, windowSize, threshold) {
  const w = scores.slice(-windowSize);
  const mean = w.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)/w.length;
  return Math.abs(mean - 0.5) > threshold;
}
```

